## 4️⃣ How Controllers Enforce Permissions

Typical pattern (example from `server/controllers/taskController.js`):

```js
import { getTaskPermissionContext } from '../utils/projectPermissions.js';

export async function updateTask(req, res) {
  const { taskId } = req.params;
  const { requesterId } = req.auth; // filled by JWT middleware

  const ctx = await getTaskPermissionContext({ taskId, requesterId });

  // Example: deny edit if member lacks the flag
  if (ctx.isMember && !ctx.settings.allow_member_edit_task) {
    const err = new Error('Permission denied: cannot edit tasks');
    err.code = 'FORBIDDEN';
    throw err;
  }

  // Owner/admin/manager shortcuts
  if (ctx.isOwner || ctx.isAdmin || ctx.isManager) {
    // allow
  }

  // ...perform the update...
}
```

All protected routes follow this pattern, guaranteeing **defense‑in‑depth**:
client UI may hide the “Edit” button, but the server will still reject a
malicious request.

